{"name":"Old World","hex":"#91a8cf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Old World","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The Eastern Hemisphere, especially Europe, Africa and Asia.","The known world before the discovery of the Americas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Old_World"}],"entries":[{"title":"Old World","blocks":[{"text":"The \"Old World\" is a term for Afro-Eurasia coined by Europeans after 1493, when they became aware of the existence of the Americas, Oceania, and Antarctica. It is used to contrast the continents of Africa, Europe, and Asia in the Eastern Hemisphere, previously thought of by the Europeans as the entire world, with the \"New World\", the newly encountered lands of the Western Hemisphere, particularly the Americas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/LocationAfricaEurasia.png/330px-LocationAfricaEurasia.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_World","wikidata":"Q174807"},{"title":"World","blocks":[{"text":"A world is a totality of entities. In philosophy, the world is customarily viewed as all of existence or the whole of reality. Some conceptions see the world as unique, while others examine the character of entities that exist in the world or talk of a plurality of worlds, challenging the idea that the world is an all-inclusive whole. The term is also commonly used as a context-dependent expression to denote a specific domain of discourse and everything within it, such as the Earth and all life on it or the whole of human existence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg/330px-Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World","wikidata":"Q16502"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Cosmographia_Claudii_Ptolomaei_ante_1467_%287455943%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Cosmographia_Claudii_Ptolomaei_ante_1467_%287455943%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Cosmographia_Claudii_Ptolomaei_ante_1467_%287455943%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Cosmographia_Claudii_Ptolomaei_ante_1467_%287455943%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Cosmographia_Claudii_Ptolomaei_ante_1467_%287455943%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Cosmographia_Claudii_Ptolomaei_ante_1467_%287455943%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map of the \"Old World\" (the 2nd-century Ptolemy world map in a 15th-century copy)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACosmographia_Claudii_Ptolomaei_ante_1467_(7455943)_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Yggdrasil.jpg/500px-Yggdrasil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Yggdrasil.jpg/500px-Yggdrasil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yggdrasil, an attempt to reconstruct the Norse world tree which connects the heavens, the world, and the underworld.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYggdrasil.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Home_in_Relation_to_Everything.png/500px-Home_in_Relation_to_Everything.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Home_in_Relation_to_Everything.png/1280px-Home_in_Relation_to_Everything.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons